# M10 — Two-Failure-Domain Continuity

Prove that one logical System preserves identity, authority, state, and effect history across two admitted nodes and failure domains.

## Why this is next

M10 `depends_on` M9 ([`sequence.v1.json`](../program/sequence.v1.json)), so it starts from a terminal single-node journey whose routes are Rust-owned and whose product proof already carries adversarial evidence. M2 supplied the membership, writer, fence, recovery, checkpoint, and removal families; M10 is where those families are exercised together against a second declared failure domain rather than in isolation. It adds provisioning/attestation of a candidate, checkpoint restore and ordered catch-up, fenced writer-epoch transition, and the operator-visible continuity projection. M11 `depends_on` M10, so useful same-system distribution stays downstream of a proven continuity floor.

