## In scope

- `M10.1` provision and attest a candidate node;
- `M10.2` admit membership and assign replica/hot-standby roles;
- `M10.3` restore a checkpoint, catch up the ordered log, verify roots and watermarks plus required outside-rollback-domain temporal/key/revocation floors, and mark readiness;
- `M10.4` inject writer, node, link, storage, witness, and restart failures;
- `M10.5` reconcile active work, perform only policy-controlled promotion, increment the writer epoch, and fence the displaced writer;
- `M10.6` replay, drain, remove, and prove declared RPO/RTO and lost-suffix handling;
- `M10.7` expose desired/observed topology, root, epoch/fence, degraded state, recovery, and continuity receipts.
- M10 owns the Operations operational-journey record (observe → promote/fence/rejoin → declare incident → recover → export), including its ordered-transition coverage obligation.
- `MEASURED`, `SYSTEM`, `AGENTGRES`, `WALLET` crossings for node attestation, epoch/floor, and checkpoint/key/revocation families; `SYSTEM`, `DAEMON`, `ENVIRONMENTS`, `SURFACES` crossings for membership/fence/promotion/rejoin/fault/RPO/RTO projection families.
- Positive, adversarial, stale-state, substitution, fault, recovery, product/operator-state, compatibility, migration, and retained-evidence work for the bounded claim, across the pulled product surfaces Systems, Operations, Environments, and Provenance.

## Out of scope

- Useful same-system distributed work, role-to-membership runtime assignment, allocation leases, and rebalance (M11).
- Federated admission, AIIP channel envelopes, portable exit, and cross-sovereign settlement (M12 and later).
- Dynamic membership, leader election, and automated failover, which remain labelled unbuilt until their own fault-model and conformance proof exists; consensus membership, threshold-authority roles, and any public-finality or L1 claim.
- The single-node terminal product journey and its claim publication (M9).

